--- a/docs/design/architecture/06-runtime-view.md
+++ b/docs/design/architecture/06-runtime-view.md
@@ -72,5 +72,9 @@ Delete is a commit-point operation too, and its cost is paid lazily, off the cri
 
 1. **Unlink (the commit point).** Removing the dirent — and tombstoning the inode/version — is a single atomic metadata mutation, linearized globally by L2 for the *name* so no zone ever resurrects a deleted file (section 8.1). The delete acknowledges here; the object is now invisible to every consistency-sensitive reader and its chunks are unreferenced.
 2. **Reclaim (custodian GC, L4).** The orphaned fragments are reclaimed by a GC custodian after a grace period — long enough that an in-flight reader holding the old version is never torn — exactly the pending-ledger sweep pattern (section 5). Reclamation is background work, not on the delete's latency path.
+   - **The orphan ledger is read one bounded window per pass, never whole.** One large retirement can install more `orphan:` marks than a single metadata `scan` may return, and a `scan` past its cap fails outright — so a whole-ledger read would stop every GC pass from then on, including the pass meant to shrink the ledger. Each pass reads at most a fixed budget of marks (65,536) through the paged `scan_page` read, resuming after the last key the previous pass read. That position is a persisted metadata record, `gc:orphan-cursor`, because the deployed loop builds a fresh GC context every pass and a leader change must not restart the walk; it is advanced only after the rest of the pass succeeds, only if it still holds the value the pass started from, and it is removed when a pass reaches the end of the ledger, so the next pass starts a new lap. A pass that read only part of the ledger never reports it settled.
+   - **A partial read only ever retains.** A fragment whose mark lies outside the window looks unmarked and is kept. The one input that reclaims on the *absence* of a mark — an expired pending lease — trusts that absence only inside the key range the window covered, and otherwise reads the fragment's own `orphan:` key, so a mark on another page still outranks the lease.
+   - **Marks with no fragment behind them are swept.** A mark whose position no server's listing reports in *this* pass — never an earlier pass's listing — is deleted once it is older than the late-write deadline `W_repoint + W_write + δ_clock` (16 s), which the deployed grace (60 s) must strictly exceed; the server checks that at compile time. A mark on a server missing from the pass's fleet is never swept. Every mark GC deletes — after reclaiming its bytes, or as a sweep — is deleted only if it still holds the value the pass judged, so a mark written again in the meantime survives. All of the pass's writes commit at most 1,000 to a transaction.
+   - **The post-restore pass** (the operator command run after the metadata restore of section 6.5) never reads the ledger whole either: it asks each unreferenced fragment's own `orphan:` key whether it is already marked, and writes a mark only if that key is still absent when the batch commits, so an existing mark's grace clock is never restarted.
 3. **Propagate across zones (L3).** If the object had replicas, the deletion propagates through the replica catalog: each holding zone drops its catalog record and its GC reclaims the local fragments. Until propagation completes, a *stale-tolerant* read in a lagging zone may still see the old object, bounded by replication lag (section 8.1); consistency-sensitive reads route to the home zone and never do.
 4. **Prove it (audit + crypto-erase).** The deletion is recorded in the append-only audit/event log — the operator's compliance and GDPR-deletion story (section 8.3). Where per-tenant envelope encryption is enabled, dropping the object/tenant key crypto-shreds the data immediately, making it unrecoverable independent of when GC runs (section 8.5).
